<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Brown_et_al./03.E:_Stoichiometry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="3.1:_Chemical_Equations"></span><h2 class="editable"><a title="Textbook_Maps/Map:_Brown_and_LeMay/03._Stoichiometry:_Calculations_with_Chemical_Formulas_and_Equations/3.1:_Chemical_Equations" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map%3A_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/03%3A_Stoichiometry-_Chemical_Formulas_and_Equations/3.01%3A_Chemical_Equations" rel="internal">3.1: Chemical Equations</a></h2>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Brown_et_al./03.E:_Stoichiometry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Conceptual_Problems"></span><h3 class="editable">Conceptual Problems</h3>

<ol>
    <li>How does a balanced chemical equation agree with the law of definite proportions?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between S<sub>8</sub> and 8S? Use this example to explain why subscripts in a formula must not be changed.</li>
    <li>What factors determine whether a chemical equation is balanced?</li>
    <li>What information can be obtained from a balanced chemical equation? Does a balanced chemical equation give information about the rate of a reaction?</li>
</ol>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Brown_et_al./03.E:_Stoichiometry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="Numerical_Problems"></span><h3 class="editable">Numerical Problems</h3>

<p>1. Balance each chemical equation.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. KI(aq) + Br<sub>2</sub>(l) &rarr; KBr(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. MnO<sub>2</sub>(s) + HCl(aq) &rarr; MnCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. Na<sub>2</sub>O(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; NaOH(aq)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. Cu(s) + AgNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) &rarr; Cu(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>(aq) + Ag(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>(aq)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. S<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>(l) + NH<sub>3</sub>(l) &rarr; S<sub>4</sub>N<sub>4</sub>(s) + S<sub>8</sub>(s) + NH<sub>4</sub>Cl(s)</p>

<p>2. Balance each chemical equation.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. Be(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; BeO(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; HNO<sub>2</sub>(aq)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. Na(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; NaOH(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. CaO(s) + HCl(aq) &rarr; CaCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. Fe(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) &rarr; FeSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p>3. Balance each chemical equation.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>(g) &rarr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. NaNO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; NaNO<sub>2</sub>(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. Al(s) + NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; N<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>3</sub>O<sub>9</sub>(l) &rarr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. reaction of butane with excess oxygen</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. IO<sub>2</sub>F(s) + BrF<sub>3</sub>(l) &rarr; IF<sub>5</sub>(l) + Br<sub>2</sub>(l) + O<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p>4. Balance each chemical equation.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. H<sub>2</sub>S(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + S<sub>8</sub>(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. KCl(aq) + HNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; KNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. NH<sub>3</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; NO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. CH<sub>4</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. NaF(aq) + Th(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>(aq) &rarr; NaNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + ThF<sub>4</sub>(s)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. Ca<sub>5</sub>(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>F(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + CaSO<sub>4</sub>&bull;2H<sub>2</sub>O(s) + HF(aq)</p>

<p>5. Balance each chemical equation.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. NaCl(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) &rarr; Na<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + HCl(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. K(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; KOH(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. reaction of octane with excess oxygen</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. S<sub>8</sub>(s) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; S<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>(l)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. CH<sub>3</sub>OH(l) + I<sub>2</sub>(s) + P<sub>4</sub>(s) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>I(l) + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>Al(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; CH<sub>4</sub>(g) + Al(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s)</p>

<p>6. Write a balanced chemical equation for each reaction.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. Aluminum reacts with bromine.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. Sodium reacts with chlorine.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. Aluminum hydroxide and acetic acid react to produce aluminum acetate and water.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. Ammonia and oxygen react to produce nitrogen monoxide and water.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. Nitrogen and hydrogen react at elevated temperature and pressure to produce ammonia.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. An aqueous solution of barium chloride reacts with a solution of sodium sulfate.</p>

<p>7. Write a balanced chemical equation for each reaction.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">a. Magnesium burns in oxygen.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">b. Carbon dioxide and sodium oxide react to produce sodium carbonate.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">c. Aluminum reacts with hydrochloric acid.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">d. An aqueous solution of silver nitrate reacts with a solution of potassium chloride.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">e. Methane burns in oxygen.</p>

<p class="mt-indent-1" style="text-align:justify;">f. Sodium nitrate and sulfuric acid react to produce sodium sulfate and nitric acid.</p>



</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_Brown_et_al./03.E:_Stoichiometry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="3.2:_Some_Simple_Patterns_of_Chemical_Reactivity"></span><h2 class="editable"><a title="Textbook_Maps/Map:_Brown_and_LeMay/03._Stoichiometry:_Calculations_with_Chemical_Formulas_and_Equations/3.2:_Some_Simple_Patterns_fo_Chemical_Reactivity" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map%3A_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/03%3A_Stoichiometry-_Chemical_Formulas_and_Equations/3.02%3A_Some_Simple_Patterns_of_Chemical_Reactivity" rel="internal">3.2: Some Simple Patterns of Chemical Reactivity</a></h2>

